\f0\i\fs22 \cf2 \CocoaLigature0 How many buildings in New Haven County, Connecticut, are located in FEMA's high-risk
\f1\i0  
\f0\i flood zone? I combined FEMA's flood maps with building footprints from Overture Maps and ran the analysis on a laptop, where the whole county takes
\f1\i0  
\f0\i under a second. Between 13,000 and 16,000 of the county's 333,000 buildings
\f1\i0  
\f0\i are in the 1%-annual-chance flood zone. The range depends on whether a building counts when any part of it touches the zone or only when its interior does; about 3,000 buildings fall in between. Along the shoreline, 460 homes are situated in FEMA's highest-risk coastal wave zone.
\f1\i0 \
\
Evaluating flood risk can be difficult when data is large, hard to access, and long processing times. Luckily, there are new ways of accessing data much quicker with modern pipelines that allow for much quicker assessments, especially for knowing whether a given property or building is in flood danger. By using a combination of parquet files and faster data processing (through duckDB), we can quickly make these assessments. 13,000\'9616,000 of 333,000 buildings in the 1% flood zone, depending on the counting rule. The range is because I ran the model in two ways, where any part of the building polygon is touching the flood zone and where the center point of the building (using center point but the point must occur in the building) is touching the zone. This analysis shows which buildings are within high flood risk zones and which ones are in high risk costal wave zones (red buildings in the map), as well as other buildings that touch the high risk flood areas (about 460 homes in the coastal wave (VE) zone) (orange buildings in the map), which are the ~3,000 edge cases. County-wide joins run in under a second on a laptop. Data: FEMA NFHL and Overture Maps, processed with DuckDB and GeoParquet. \
  \
